.\" cyk(1) - bytebeat synthesizer and oscilloscope for the terminal
.TH CYK 1 "October 20, 2026" "cyk" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cyk \- bytebeat synthesizer and oscilloscope for the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cyk
.RB [ \-l ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR hz ]
.RB [ \-a
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-w
.I file
.RB [ \-d
.IR sec ]]
.RB [ \-s
.IR cols\fRx\fIrows ]
.RI [ formula ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A bytebeat is a program in a single arithmetic formula: evaluated 8,000 times
a second as a sample counter
.I t
increments, its low byte is a sample of sound.
Simple bitwise operations and integer arithmetic turn counting into music:
rhythms, melodies, arpeggios, and drones.
.PP
.B cyk
evaluates bytebeat formulas and renders their sound and shape in the terminal
in real time.
The screen shows the formula, the sample counter
.IR t ,
the playback sample rate, three live knobs
.IR a ,
.IR b ,
and
.IR c ,
and a visual waveform display.
.PP
The display supports two visualizer modes, toggled with
.BR v :
a Unicode braille oscilloscope that connects consecutive sample points
with 2\(mu4 dot cells (U+2800 through U+28FF) to show continuous wave dynamics,
and an 8-bit bit-plane waterfall showing the raw binary bits of each sample
over time.
.PP
Formulas can be edited live while the sound plays.
Pressing
.B e
or
.B /
opens the editor on the bottom row.
As each character is typed, the formula is compiled.
If a formula is broken while typing, the place where it failed is underlined,
while the previous formula plays on.
Pressing
.B Enter
switches to the new formula with no click or pause;
.B Esc
cancels the edit and keeps the playing formula.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-preset " " \fIn\fR
Select built-in preset tune
.I n
(1 to 10) at startup.
Default is 1.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-rate " " \fIhz\fR
Playback sample rate in Hertz.
Default is 8000.
.TP
.BI \-a " n"
Set the initial integer value for live knob
.IR a .
Overrides the preset default.
.TP
.BI \-b " n"
Set the initial integer value for live knob
.IR b .
Overrides the preset default.
.TP
.BI \-c " n"
Set the initial integer value for live knob
.IR c .
Overrides the preset default.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-wav " " \fIfile\fR
Render audio directly to a standard 8-bit unsigned mono RIFF WAVE file and exit
without opening the terminal interface.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-duration " " \fIsec\fR
Duration in seconds of audio to export when writing a WAV file with
.BR \-w .
Default is 10.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-screen " " \fIcols\fRx\fIrows\fR
Override terminal screen dimensions to
.I cols
columns by
.I rows
rows for rendering.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List built-in preset tunes, authors, default knob settings, and formulas,
then exit.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-audio
Run the visualizer in the terminal without outputting sound.
Useful in environments without audio hardware or over remote connections.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show command-line options and exit.
.SH KEYS
.SS Normal Mode
.TP
.BR n ", " Right
Next preset tune.
.TP
.BR p ", " Left
Previous preset tune.
.TP
.BR 1 " \(en " 0
Select preset tune 1 to 10 directly (0 chooses preset 10).
.TP
.B Space
Pause or resume audio playback and oscilloscope animation.
.TP
.B r
Reset the sample counter
.I t
to zero.
.TP
.B v
Toggle visualizer between the braille oscilloscope and the 8-bit bit-plane
waterfall.
.TP
.BR a ", " A
Increment or decrement knob
.IR a .
.TP
.BR b ", " B
Increment or decrement knob
.IR b .
.TP
.BR c ", " C
Increment or decrement knob
.IR c .
.TP
.BR + ", " \-
Increase or decrease the playback sample rate.
.TP
.B .
Advance playback by one frame while paused.
.TP
.BR e ", " /
Enter live formula editing mode.
.TP
.B w
Write the current formula audio to a WAV file.
.TP
.BR q ", " Esc
Quit
.BR cyk .
.SS Edit Mode
.TP
.B Backspace
Delete the character before the cursor.
.TP
.B C-u
Clear the formula line.
.TP
.B Enter
Accept the edited formula, update playback seamlessly, and return to normal
mode.
.TP
.B Esc
Cancel editing, restore the previous formula, and return to normal mode.
.SH FORMULAS
A bytebeat formula is an arithmetic expression over integer values.
Expressions are evaluated using 64-bit signed arithmetic.
The lowest 8 bits of the resulting integer form an unsigned 8-bit PCM audio
sample (0 to 255).
.SS Operators
Operators are listed from highest to lowest precedence:
.TP
.BR \- ", " ~ ", " !
Unary prefix operators: arithmetic negation, bitwise NOT (one's complement),
and logical NOT (yielding 1 if zero, and 0 otherwise).
.TP
.BR * ", " / ", " %
Multiplication, division, and modulo (remainder).
Division and modulo by zero yield 0 rather than terminating execution or
trapping.
Dividing the minimum 64-bit signed integer by \-1 also yields 0 to prevent
arithmetic overflow.
.TP
.BR + ", " \-
Addition and subtraction.
.TP
.BR << ", " >>
Bitwise left and right shift.
The shift count is masked to its low 6 bits (0 to 63).
.TP
.BR < ", " <= ", " > ", " >=
Relational comparisons: less than, less than or equal, greater than, and
greater than or equal.
Evaluate to 1 if true, and 0 otherwise.
.TP
.BR == ", " !=
Equality and inequality comparisons.
Evaluate to 1 if true, and 0 otherwise.
.TP
.B &
Bitwise AND.
.TP
.B ^
Bitwise XOR.
.TP
.B |
Bitwise OR.
.PP
Parentheses
.BR ( )
group subexpressions and override default operator precedence.
.SS Variables
.TP
.B t
The sample counter.
Starts at zero and increments by one for each audio sample generated (by default,
8,000 times a second).
.TP
.BR a ", " b ", " c
Live parameter knobs.
Each knob holds a 64-bit integer, modified during playback with keyboard
controls or initialized on the command line.
.PP
Variable names are case-insensitive:
.BR t ,
.BR a ,
.BR b ,
.BR c ,
.BR T ,
.BR A ,
.BR B ,
and
.B C
are accepted.
.SS Constants
Constants can be written in decimal
.RB ( 42 ,
.BR 8000 )
or in hexadecimal with a
.B 0x
or
.B 0X
prefix
.RB ( 0x2a ,
.BR 0xff ).
.SH EXAMPLES
Start the interactive synthesizer with the default preset tune:
.RS 4
.B cyk
.RE
.PP
Play preset 3 with knob
.I a
tuned to 7:
.RS 4
.B cyk \-p 3 \-a 7
.RE
.PP
Play a custom formula directly from the command line:
.RS 4
.B cyk \(dqt * (42 & t >> 10)\(dq
.RE
.PP
Play a custom formula with initial knob values:
.RS 4
.B cyk \-a 5 \-b 9 \(dqt * (a & t >> b)\(dq
.RE
.PP
Export 10 seconds of preset 2 to an 8-bit mono WAV file:
.RS 4
.B cyk \-p 2 \-w crowd.wav \-d 10
.RE
.PP
Export 15 seconds of a custom formula to a WAV file without terminal display:
.RS 4
.B cyk \-w space.wav \-d 15 \(dq((t * (t >> 8 | t >> 9) & 46 & t >> 8)) ^ (t & t >> 13 | t >> 6)\(dq
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR raz (1)
